    "body": "## Summary\n\nImplements **ADR-025: External CSS Loading** — external stylesheets (`<link rel=\"stylesheet\">`) are now fetched, parsed, and applied alongside inline `<style>` CSS.\n\n**Verified visually on Wikipedia's HTML article: the article body is now readable.** Sans-serif typography, proper line-height, styled inline links, formatted references. This is the \"HoneyBee actually browses\" moment the ADR-018 → 024 arc has been building toward.\n\nThree commits. First time the browser loads real-world CSS from a CDN.\n\n## The visible evidence\n\nArticle-body transformation (screenshots in this session):\n\n| Feature | Before ADR-025 | After ADR-025 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Body font | Default serif | **Sans-serif** |\n| Line-height | Default | **~1.6 (reading-optimized)** |\n| Inline links | Blue, no underline | **Styled + underlined** |\n| Heading hierarchy | Uniform | **H2/H3 sized** |\n| Citation markers `[a]`, `[3]` | Plain text | **Superscripted** |\n| References section | Unformatted list | **Numbered, hanging indents, backlink markers** |\n| Sidebar link colors | Default blue | **Dark (partial improvement)** |\n| Sidebar column layout | Blob | Still blob (blocked by `@media`) |\n| Header layout | Text row | Text row (blocked by `@media`) |\n| TOC layout | Unstyled list | Unstyled list (blocked by `@media`) |\n\n**Escape hatch from ADR-025's Verification section did NOT fire.** The ADR specified: \"if <2 of 3 expected-YES checks pass, ship anyway but document the smaller delta.\" Two expected-YES checks (article body, references) passed cleanly — plus partial sidebar improvement above prediction.\n\n## The surprise finding (worth documenting)\n\nADR-025's Context measured **27.0% parser acceptance** (390 of 1,443 rules from Wikipedia's CSS). Predicted: \"article content styling improves... does NOT deliver Wikipedia-looks-like-Wikipedia.\"\n\n**Actual visual improvement: ~40-50%.** The prediction was too conservative.\n\n**Why:** the parseable 27% concentrated in **article-content** rules — typography, spacing, colors, heading hierarchy, list indentation. The unparseable 73% is dominated by **layout** rules (columns, positioning, responsive breakpoints). Layout rules determine structural look; content rules determine readability. The 27% happened to capture almost all of the readability win.\n\nSame class of finding as ADR-023's \"2,500 `<a>` per wrapper, not 10\" — **acceptance rates alone don't predict user-visible impact.** Visual verification revealed the concentration; the parser number did not.\n\nDocumented as a distinct subsection in ADR-025's Implementation section.\n\n## Commits\n\n| Commit | Scope |\n|---|---|\n| `6ab6c47` | `collect_css_sources` helper + `PageCache` extension to `CacheEntry { html, external_css }` with backward-compatible shims. 15 new tests. |\n| `1f9b739` | `RenderPipeline::render_with_css` API + window fetch loop (caps: 10 sheets, 5s) + cache-hit path re-renders from `(html, css)`. 2 new tests. |\n| `506da00` | Docs: ADR-025 status → Implemented, README, CHANGELOG, measured delta + surprise finding |\n\n## Design decisions worth reviewing\n\n- **Synchronous fetch** — ~500ms-1s extra on Wikipedia navigation. Feels like part of navigation (HTML already blocks 1-3s). ADR's \"async if unacceptable\" trigger did not fire. Async deferred to a future ADR.\n- **Cache extends from string → `CacheEntry { html, external_css }`** — Back/Forward re-render from cache (no re-fetch). Backward-compatible surface preserved for `window.cpp`'s two call sites.\n- **Concatenate external + inline CSS, parse as one sheet** — preserves source-order cascade. Multi-sheet support deferred.\n- **Fail-graceful per sheet** — a page with 4 of 5 sheets is better than nothing.\n- **Protocol-relative URLs** (`//host/path`) inherit scheme from base URL.\n- **Entity decoding is idempotent** — the ADR's flagged gotcha (Wikipedia's hrefs contain `&amp;`) is handled regardless of whether `get_attribute` returns raw or pre-decoded.\n\n## Test count\n\n- Before ADR-025: 271 cases, 1185 assertions\n- After Commit 1: 286 cases, 1232 assertions (+15 from css_sources + CacheEntry)\n- After Commit 2: **288 cases, 1239 assertions** (+2 render_with_css tests)\n- Zero warnings, zero failures\n\n## Verification checklist\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Article body typography (sans-serif, line-height) | ✅ |\n| Inline link styling | ✅ |\n| Heading hierarchy | ✅ |\n| Citation markers superscripted | ✅ |\n| References formatted | ✅ |\n| Sidebar link colors | ✅ (partial) |\n| Sidebar layout | ❌ (deferred to ADR-027) |\n| Header / TOC layout | ❌ (deferred to ADR-027) |\n| No regression on inline-`<style>`-only pages | ✅ (all 288 tests pass) |\n| Back/Forward from cache works | ✅ (existing tests + cache-hit path tested) |\n| Sync fetch completes within caps on Wikipedia | ✅ (2 sheets, well under 10/5s) |\n\n## Not included (deferred, three-ADR continuation)\n\n| ADR | Unlocks |\n|---|---|\n| **ADR-026** | `:root` + `var()` — 255 accepted rules become visible once CSS variables resolve. Likely unlocks sidebar/link colors more completely. |\n| **ADR-027** | `@media` blocks — 72 blocks. Almost certainly the Vector skin column/layout rules. Unlocks sidebar structure, header, TOC. |\n| **ADR-028** | `:not()` + attribute operators — 391 + ~150 rules. Polish. |\n\nPlus `::before`/`::after` (98 rules, decorative, needs pseudo-element render pipeline), `@import`, async fetch, CORS, media-type filtering.\n\n## ADR\n\n`docs/decisions/025-external-css-loading.md` — status now **Implemented**, with Implementation section documenting the measured visual delta and the 27% → 40-50% surprise finding.\n\nRefs #153\n",
